<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Amathus</h1><p class="st-meta">1050 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.) · capital Amathus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Cypriot kingdom on the south coast that kept the island's pre-Greek Eteocypriot language alive into the 4th century BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Amathus was founded c. 1050 BCE on a hill near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limassol">Limassol</a>. Ancient writers regarded its people as autochthonous Cypriots, and the city preserved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eteocypriot-language">Eteocypriot language</a>, written in the Cypriot syllabary, alongside Greek. It also had close links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, and it was probably among the Cypriot kingdoms that paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> in the 7th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The acropolis held a palace and a major sanctuary of the Cypriot goddess later called Aphrodite, with a giant stone vase now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. The kingdom grew rich from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and agriculture. It was loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>: in 498 BCE it refused to join the revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onesilus">Onesilus of Salamis</a> and was besieged.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>King Androcles sent ships to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a> in 332 BCE. The monarchy was ended under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> c. 312 BCE, and Amathus continued as a city under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It declined after the 7th century CE and was abandoned.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1050 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:38.81%;width:14.69%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1050 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amathus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Androcles</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cypriot religion; cult of Aphrodite</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Eteocypriot; Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Cyprus</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Amathus</dd></dl></aside></div>

